1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,960
you'll probably have tenure before you would even get your

2
00:00:01,960 --> 00:00:04,200
first job in history.

3
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,160
But if someone were to say, should I do a PhD at all,

4
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:09,440
that's where I would go to, well, do you love this stuff?

5
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:13,440
If yes, then I think it's a very good deal.

6
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:18,200
Otherwise, no, I would not recommend it.

7
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,880
So what about if you are a civil servant, you're just

8
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:24,120
here for the pay grade bump from getting the credential?

9
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:29,280
Yeah.

10
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:31,800
Probably not.

11
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:33,400
But it's OK.

12
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:35,240
But it seems like there's way easier ways of doing it.

13
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:40,960
It's actually, if you want to just do it for the civil

14
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:43,600
service pay grade bump, that you might want to just do the

15
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:45,880
easiest graduate degree you can find.

16
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:50,680
Because normally, at least in the military, your pay has

17
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:53,240
something to do with your degree, but nothing to do with

18
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:55,120
your major.

19
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:58,920
So you can get the same pay grade bump in the Air Force

20
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:01,960
from getting a history degree or something, although those

21
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:03,640
degrees do take forever to finish.

22
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:05,560
And the military normally says, if you don't finish in

23
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:07,920
three years, we all do that.

24
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:11,160
So anyway, that's a weird case.

25
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:14,160
But anyway, I would say the reason to be here is because

26
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:15,840
you love the subject and you are dying to

27
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:17,120
work in the subject.

28
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:20,560
And otherwise, I will say it's not a bad path, but I don't

29
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:22,800
think it's a good idea for you to be here.

30
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:23,280
Yeah.

31
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:26,680
And this means you should count the utility value.

32
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:26,960
Yeah.

33
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:28,960
So when you're doing it, of course, you can put a dollar

34
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:30,760
value on your job satisfaction.

35
00:01:30,760 --> 00:01:34,520
So P. Becky likes to say that he made more money as a tennis

36
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:40,240
instructor than he did for his first 10 years in economics.

37
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:41,720
He's a really good tennis player.

38
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:45,160
He was like the Grand Timer of the world.

39
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:47,440
And he was in a rich part of the country, too, where people

40
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:51,040
would pay $200 an hour for their kid to go and learn from

41
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:54,840
a guy as good as he was.

42
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:56,400
OK.

43
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:01,120
Now, notice, so there's a very simple policy governments

44
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:04,000
could use to improve the market's efficiency.

45
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,560
Again, of course, step one is just cut the subsidies.

46
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:09,160
But if that's not enough, the governments could actually tax

47
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:10,280
education.

48
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:12,680
So we can innocently model education

49
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:14,320
race-reel resources.

50
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:17,440
And the idea of the tax is that it would preserve the

51
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:19,360
relative ranking while discouraging

52
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:20,600
the actual activity.

53
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:23,680
Now, you might remember I've talked about other cases where

54
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:25,640
there's a market failure.

55
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:27,120
And there's something government could do.

56
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:29,280
And then actual governments do exactly the opposite of the

57
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:31,640
thing that they could do and improve things.

58
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:33,880
Well, here's another case.

59
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:36,600
Just like there is something government could do to deal

60
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:37,400
with adverse selection.

61
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:42,680
And that is to require insurance, but allow for

62
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:45,760
insurers to charge whatever they want based upon risk.

63
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:47,640
In reality, governments usually try to stop.

64
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:50,720
Insurance companies from charging whatever they want

65
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:51,720
based on risk.

66
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:54,480
Similarly, there is something that governments could do to

67
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:55,920
go and reduce the signaling problem.

68
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:56,920
And that's tax education.

69
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:59,280
And there's what every known government actually does,

70
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:02,560
which is to subsidize education.

71
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:05,120
So you don't need to have a dogmatic view that markets

72
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:07,320
work perfectly to still say that government

73
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:09,040
policy's terrible.

74
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:11,480
But again, this is like subsidizing pollution.

75
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:14,200
In fact, in the signaling model, signaling is just like

76
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:14,960
pollution.

77
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:18,040
It's just a way where you make yourself better off while

78
00:03:18,040 --> 00:03:19,280
impoverishing the world.

79
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:25,880
So the idea here then is if education were unsubsidized,

80
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:28,240
you might not be able to afford it.

81
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:31,760
But you also wouldn't need it to get a good job anymore.

82
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:34,640
So when people are freaking out at me on radio shows, and

83
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:37,320
like, do you want to deprive the poor of a chance to get

84
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:39,440
education, is it what I really want to do?

85
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:42,000
I want to restore to the poor the ability to get a good job

86
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:43,840
while still being able to afford it.

87
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:48,640
Or the ability to get a good job right out of high school,

88
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:51,760
which is a lot more relevant for poor people than your

89
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:57,120
thing, which benefits only the most successful.

90
00:03:57,120 --> 00:04:01,720
So there is this odd focus on the rare, super talented kid

91
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:04,800
from a poor family, rather than the much larger number

92
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:06,360
that don't do well in school.

93
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:09,200
And today, I have trouble getting a job as a secretary

94
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:11,520
or a waiter at a good restaurant because of

95
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:12,720
credential inflation.

96
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:15,440
I did talk about credential inflation a bit when I started

97
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:16,080
signaling, right?

98
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:20,120
But again, this is a telltale sign of signaling when for one

99
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:24,440
and the same actual job, you need a lot more education to

100
00:04:24,440 --> 00:04:28,960
be considered worthy of training, which really is

101
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:30,960
ubiquitous throughout the modern economy.

102
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:34,000
Jobs that in 1950, you would get right out of high school,

103
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,720
maybe even out of middle school.

104
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:37,760
You say, yeah, are you 14?

105
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:38,480
You want to be a waiter?

106
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:39,000
Sure, right?

107
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,240
Here's how you do it.

108
00:04:41,240 --> 00:04:43,840
Whereas today, someone with the same credentials would

109
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:45,400
have their application thrown away.

110
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:46,200
It would be a joke.

111
00:04:46,200 --> 00:04:47,360
They say, well, we're not going to consider

112
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:49,560
someone like that.

113
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:54,480
In a world where 80% of people finish high school, then if

114
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:57,640
you haven't, you put yourself in the bottom 20%.

115
00:04:57,640 --> 00:04:59,160
And there's a lot of jobs where people are happy to

116
00:04:59,160 --> 00:05:01,000
consider someone in the 40th percentile, where they don't

117
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,600
want to consider someone in the 10th percentile.

118
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:06,040
Useless idea.

119
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:09,520
So in a world where people are doing a lot less education,

120
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:12,480
people would switch to other methods, which are a lot less

121
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:16,200
wasteful, like apprenticing and on-the-job learning, and so

122
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:18,240
on, right?

123
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:20,600
So basically, the kind of economy that we have now, for

124
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:24,600
the most part, is one where you stay in school for a long

125
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:28,760
time, learning almost nothing that you'll ever use again.

126
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:31,880
But finally, at the end, you have the certification to get

127
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:34,560
employers to take you seriously enough to train you

128
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:37,360
for a job that really is quite menial, all

129
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:39,200
things considered.

130
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:41,360
I remember my best friend at Princeton had a very academic

131
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:43,760
personality, but he did not get an academic job.

132
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:46,240
Instead, he got a job in economic consulting.

133
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:49,200
And how intellectually demanding did he find

134
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:51,680
economic consulting?

135
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:55,200
Not very intellectually demanding at all.

136
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:58,160
A lot of his job just consisted in calling up firms

137
00:05:58,160 --> 00:05:59,800
in a business and asking them some questions about their

138
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:03,120
business, and then writing up a report.

139
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:04,320
That was his job.

140
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:07,840
But he easily could have done it.

141
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:09,480
Right after undergraduate, he could have done it.

142
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:11,200
And again, this guy was a math genius, so he could have

143
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:13,560
easily done it out of high school, really.

144
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:15,200
And he'd say, well, maybe you wouldn't have been socially

145
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:16,880
polished enough to make the phone calls.

146
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:18,840
Fine, give him a one-month training session on how to

147
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:20,320
make a phone call.

148
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:21,280
That's all he really needed.

149
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:23,160
In fact, probably he was terrible at making phone calls

150
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:25,240
out of graduate school, going like that.

151
00:06:25,240 --> 00:06:26,640
So he never mentioned the training.

152
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:30,120
But my guess is that he had a bunch of really awkward phone

153
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:32,000
calls at first, because he'd never done it.

154
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:37,680
And then he learned how to do it by doing it.

155
00:06:37,680 --> 00:06:42,520
The way that almost everybody learns almost everything.

156
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:47,480
I always think about when finally, I think my kids are

157
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:49,480
like in third grade, and my wife is still showering them.

158
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:50,480
I'm like, enough's enough.

159
00:06:50,480 --> 00:06:52,960
I'm going to teach them how to shower themselves.

160
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:53,600
And I get there, right?

161
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:54,840
I'm a professor.

162
00:06:54,840 --> 00:06:55,440
What am I going to do?

163
00:06:55,440 --> 00:06:58,400
Give them electron showering theory?

164
00:06:58,400 --> 00:06:59,920
No.

165
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:00,520
Get in.

166
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:01,920
Do as I do.

167
00:07:01,920 --> 00:07:02,800
Do this.

168
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:03,440
Do this.

169
00:07:03,440 --> 00:07:05,360
And I made up a mnemonic song.

170
00:07:05,360 --> 00:07:06,520
Do this.

171
00:07:06,520 --> 00:07:07,440
Wash your arm.

172
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:08,640
Wash your arm.

173
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:10,000
Fully behaviorist.

174
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:11,000
No theory.

175
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,200
Totally anti-intellectual.

176
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:16,960
But the only thing is, and then correcting them in real time.

177
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:19,880
So they're washing their leg with two fingers.

178
00:07:19,880 --> 00:07:21,040
Like, no, don't do that.

179
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:21,760
Two fingers?

180
00:07:21,760 --> 00:07:22,240
No.

181
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:22,960
Full hand.

182
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:23,440
Like this.

183
00:07:23,440 --> 00:07:24,200
Like a paintbrush.

184
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:26,160
Your hand is a paintbrush.

185
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:27,920
Not that helpful, because they'd also never used a

186
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:29,240
paintbrush.

187
00:07:29,240 --> 00:07:29,840
Like this.

188
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:30,560
Like this.

189
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:32,120
Do this.

190
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:37,400
And you go through this drill 20 times, and fully effective.

191
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:40,640
And I could have given the shower theory lecture 20 times,

192
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:43,600
and what good would that have ever done?

193
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:44,320
And you.

194
00:07:44,320 --> 00:07:45,480
You really pay attention to the world.

195
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:46,960
This is how things actually work.

196
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:49,720
There's a whole literature in psychology on expertise.

197
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:52,520
How does anyone get good at anything?

198
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:56,320
Practice, practice, practice.

199
00:07:56,320 --> 00:07:56,920
So let's see.

200
00:07:56,920 --> 00:08:01,040
Are you familiar with Malcolm Gladwell's book, Outliers?

201
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:03,040
Yes, so this is heavily based upon the work of a

202
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:06,720
psychologist named K. Anders Erickson.

203
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:10,680
All right, now, Erickson overstates the case for

204
00:08:10,680 --> 00:08:15,280
practice, and he literally seems to say that the only

205
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:18,000
thing anyone needs to get up to the level of the best

206
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,400
person on earth is to practice as much as the best person on

207
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:24,080
earth, and Gladwell then repeats it.

208
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:26,000
And this is not a case where Gladwell

209
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:27,920
misunderstood Erickson.

210
00:08:27,920 --> 00:08:31,360
Erickson had multiple statements, very explicitly,

211
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:34,160
that seemed to say that the reason why the Beatles became

212
00:08:34,160 --> 00:08:35,280
the number one band in the world is because they

213
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:37,480
practiced more than any other band on earth.

214
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:40,200
It's not like you have data on all the other bands.

215
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:42,800
But no one tried that hard and still failed.

216
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:44,480
But anyway, there's a follow-up literature showing

217
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:46,600
that practice is important, but a bunch of other obvious

218
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:48,800
things are important, too.

219
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:52,920
But nevertheless, if you just reinterpret the Malcolm

220
00:08:52,920 --> 00:08:57,080
Gladwell theory as, it's not a theory of excellence.

221
00:08:57,080 --> 00:08:59,240
It's a theory of improvement.

222
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:02,120
It doesn't say that practice will make anybody great.

223
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:03,400
That's wrong.

224
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:04,880
There's a lot of people who practice a lot, and they're

225
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:06,720
still not very good.

226
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:08,920
But if it's a theory of how does anyone improve at

227
00:09:08,920 --> 00:09:11,720
anything, then the practice theory is awesome and

228
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:16,360
explains almost everything we see.

229
00:09:16,360 --> 00:09:18,800
There's almost nothing that is intellectually hard or

230
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:21,400
physically hard where the people that are good at it

231
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:23,240
haven't done a lot of practice.

232
00:09:23,240 --> 00:09:25,760
And there's almost none where anyone is really good right

233
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:26,960
out the bat.

234
00:09:26,960 --> 00:09:29,840
So there are people who are chess grandmasters only a

235
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:32,240
couple thousand hours of practice.

236
00:09:32,240 --> 00:09:33,280
But think about it.

237
00:09:33,280 --> 00:09:38,240
Well, you only practice for a couple thousand hours.

238
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:40,000
Even Mozart.

239
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
That kid was practicing from such an early age.

240
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:44,880
Also, a lot of people think his dad actually wrote a lot

241
00:09:44,880 --> 00:09:47,120
of his earlier stuff, which is plausible.

242
00:09:47,120 --> 00:09:50,160
But anyway.

243
00:09:50,160 --> 00:09:52,480
So this is great stuff.

244
00:09:52,480 --> 00:09:54,800
But just think about this workup practice as a theory of

245
00:09:54,800 --> 00:09:57,800
how people actually improve at stuff, and then it makes

246
00:09:57,800 --> 00:09:58,880
great sense.

247
00:09:58,880 --> 00:10:00,960
I'll actually say that I even fell into this mistake in my

248
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:01,720
first year of grad school.

249
00:10:01,720 --> 00:10:04,320
You know I practiced for all of my tests until the

250
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:06,600
comprehensive exams?

251
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:08,400
By studying the notes.

252
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:09,800
I went and reread the notes.

253
00:10:09,800 --> 00:10:10,600
And that's what I did.

254
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:14,800
And I got C's on all the major exams.

255
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:17,040
And you know what other students were doing?

256
00:10:17,040 --> 00:10:17,720
The sensible thing.

257
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:19,960
They got old exams and practiced them.

258
00:10:19,960 --> 00:10:21,800
And you know what I finally did?

259
00:10:21,800 --> 00:10:23,400
I finally said, you know what?

260
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:26,720
After a year of doing very poorly, maybe I should do

261
00:10:26,720 --> 00:10:28,200
something else.

262
00:10:28,200 --> 00:10:30,360
What if I were to get old exams and practice them for a

263
00:10:30,360 --> 00:10:33,000
month?

264
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:33,960
I don't feel like it.

265
00:10:33,960 --> 00:10:37,280
